          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Franklin's Views on Slavery and Abolition</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Franklin's views on slavery changed significantly. He owned slaves in the 1730s but by the 1780s, he had freed his slaves and became an outspoken abolitionist. He became president of the Pennsylvania Society for Promoting the Abolition of Slavery and worked to end the practice, which he saw as a moral wrong.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nFranklin was known for his wit, wisdom, and diplomatic skill. He lived abroad for many years but always considered Philadelphia his home. He received honorary degrees from Harvard and Yale and was a fellow of the Royal Society of London. Franklin died on April 17, 1790, and left a lasting legacy as a statesman, inventor, and champion of freedom and enlightenment values.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Remembering Franklin's Impact</strong><br>\nFranklin's influence extends beyond his lifetime and the borders of the nation he helped to found. Schools, institutions, and awards bear his name, celebrating his contributions to science, literature, and governance. His face is on the hundred-dollar bill, a testament to his enduring place in American history and the respect he commands as one of the country's founding fathers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
